        {
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "The peak positions index on a primitive tetragonal cell, with representative assignments:\n\n- 9.66\u00b0 \u2192 (100)\n- 13.26\u00b0 \u2192 (001)\n- 13.68\u00b0 \u2192 (110)\n- 16.43\u00b0 \u2192 (101)\n- 19.09\u00b0 \u2192 (111)\n- 19.39\u00b0 \u2192 (200)\n- 25.52\u00b0 \u2192 (211)\n- 26.70\u00b0 \u2192 (002)\n- 27.56\u00b0 \u2192 (220)\n- 29.26\u00b0 \u2192 (300)\n\nThis gives approximately \\(a=9.149\\) \u00c5 and \\(c=6.671\\) \u00c5. The chemically plausible primary assignment is the non-centrosymmetric tetragonal **P-4** Ca\u2084SmO(BO\u2083)\u2083 structure, \\(Z=2\\). The model contains one tetrahedral BO\u2084 and two trigonal BO\u2083 groups per formula unit.\n\nThe fractional coordinates below are a symmetry- and geometry-regularized structural model. They should be finalized by whole-pattern Rietveld refinement, particularly because peak positions alone do not uniquely distinguish P-4 from lower-symmetry primitive tetragonal alternatives.\n\n```cif\ndata_Ca4SmB3O10_primary\n_audit_creation_method\n'Indexed from supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern; prototype-constrained model'\n\n_chemical_name_common                 'calcium samarium oxyborate'\n_chemical_formula_sum                 'Ca4 Sm B3 O10'\n_chemical_formula_structural          'Ca4SmO(BO3)3'\n_cell_formula_units_Z                 2\n\n_cell_length_a                        9.1490\n_cell_length_b                        9.1490\n_cell_length_c                        6.6710\n_cell_angle_alpha                     90\n_cell_angle_beta                      90\n_cell_angle_gamma                     90\n_cell_volume                          558.40\n\n_exptl_crystal_density_diffrn         2.99\n_diffrn_radiation_type                'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength          1.5406\n\n_space_group_crystal_system           tetragonal\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt             'P -4'\n_space_group_IT_number                81\n_space_group_name_Hall                '-P 4'\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1  'x,y,z'\n2  '-y,x,-z'\n3  '-x,-y,z'\n4  'y,-x,-z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_B_iso_or_equiv\nSm1  Sm  2  g  0.0000  0.5000  0.2350  1.0  0.60\nCa1  Ca  4  h  0.1200  0.2400  0.0400  1.0  0.75\nCa2  Ca  4  h  0.3100  0.0800  0.7800  1.0  0.75\nB1   B   2  g  0.0000  0.5000  0.7350  1.0  0.80\nB2   B   4  h  0.2850  0.1650  0.3600  1.0  0.80\nO1   O   4  h  0.1050  0.5750  0.8350  1.0  0.90\nO2   O   4  h  0.0750  0.6050  0.6350  1.0  0.90\nO3   O   4  h  0.4350  0.1650  0.3600  1.0  0.90\nO4   O   4  h  0.2100  0.2950  0.3600  1.0  0.90\nO5   O   4  h  0.2100  0.0350  0.3600  1.0  0.90\n```\n\nThe metric assignment is considerably more secure than the unrefined fractional coordinates. P4 and P-4m2 are the principal space-group alternatives permitted by the indexed reflection positions, but no separate alternative coordinate model is justified without a profile refinement.",
